---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { RATING_PRIOR_WEIGHT } from "@linux-statistics/shared";
---

<Base
  title="How community scores work | LinuxStatistics"
  description="How LinuxStatistics turns user ratings into a distro score: a smoothed average that resists a few extreme ratings, with the verified share shown."
>
  <article class="page prose">
    <h1>How community scores work</h1>
    <p class="lede">Ratings come from signed-in users. One rating per person per distro; you can change or remove it any time.</p>
    <h2>The score is a smoothed average</h2>
    <p>
      A distro with two ratings shouldn't be able to beat one with two hundred. So every score starts at the average of
      all ratings on the site and moves toward the distro's own average as its ratings come in. The formula is{" "}
      <code>(C × site average + sum of ratings) ÷ (C + number of ratings)</code>, with C = {RATING_PRIOR_WEIGHT}. After
      about {RATING_PRIOR_WEIGHT} ratings a distro's own ratings outweigh the site average. Each distro page also shows
      the plain average.
    </p>
    <h2>Verified ratings</h2>
    <p>
      Ratings from accounts signed in through GitHub, GitLab or Codeberg count straight away, and the forge account has to
      be at least 30 days old. Each distro page shows what share of its ratings come from these verified accounts.
    </p>
    <h2>Moderation</h2>
    <p>Reviews appear immediately. We hide spam and abuse after the fact. Sponsors and affiliates never change a score.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
